### Interpretation across dataset variants

The progression from **basic** to **group shuffle**, **solvent descriptors**,
and **all entries** changes both the evaluation split and, in later variants,
the available data and features. The scores therefore describe the specific
dataset-and-split combinations and they are not a controlled feature-only
ablation.

1. **Basic (row-wise):** Random Forest and Extra Trees report very low test
   errors (MAE 0.132 and 0.107; RMSE 0.273 and 0.302) and high \(R^2\) values
   (0.957 and 0.948). These results are not directly comparable with the
   following pair-grouped results: a row-wise split can put observations from
   the same molecule-solvent pair in both training and test sets. The model can
   consequently benefit from datasets including only a temperature change,
   making this a less demanding estimate of generalization. CatBoost's basic
   score (MAE 0.452, RMSE 0.604, \(R^2=0.792\)) is weaker than the two tree
   ensembles on this split but still shows better values. The row-wise
   advantage should not be interpreted as a uniform improvement across model
   families.
2. **Group shuffle:** Grouping molecule-solvent pairs removes this bias by not
   having similar data in both training and validation data, resulting in a
   stricter estimate for unseen pairs. All three models show a substantial
   increase in error relative to their row-wise result where that comparison
   is available. Random Forest performs best here (MAE 0.483, RMSE 0.669,
   \(R^2=0.722\)), followed by Extra Trees (0.512, 0.687, 0.706) and CatBoost
   (0.529, 0.694, 0.701). The drop is consistent with the row-wise split
   benefiting from pair overlap. The split does not by itself establish how the
   models perform on entirely unseen molecules, since grouping is by pair.
3. **Solvent descriptors:** Adding explicit solvent descriptors improves
   Random Forest relative to its grouped result (MAE 0.450, RMSE 0.640,
   \(R^2=0.746\)), and improves CatBoost (MAE 0.520, RMSE 0.687,
   \(R^2=0.706\)). Extra Trees instead worsens on all three measures (MAE
   0.537, RMSE 0.712, \(R^2=0.685\)), however these changes are very minimal.
   Thus the additional solvent features are not uniformly beneficial: their
   value depends on the learner and the sampled test pairs.
4. **All entries:** Extending coverage to all supported entries provides more
   observations (about 101,000 rows rather than about 64,800 in the filtered
   datasets), but does not improve every model. Random Forest has the lowest
   MAE of its grouped variants (0.412), and Extra Trees also improves over its
   group-shuffle result (MAE 0.433, RMSE 0.639, \(R^2=0.723\)). For CatBoost,
   all-entry results are slightly worse than its group-shuffle results (MAE
   0.505, RMSE 0.691, \(R^2=0.676\)) and do not surpass its solvent-descriptor
   result. More training rows alone therefore do not guarantee better
   generalization; the broader entry set also changes the data distribution
   and the composition of the held-out pairs.

Across the pair-grouped variants, Random Forest is the strongest overall
performer on this holdout: it has the lowest MAE and RMSE for group shuffle,
solvent descriptors, and all entries, as well as the highest \(R^2\) for the
first two. Extra Trees is competitive on all entries, while CatBoost does not
lead any of the grouped comparisons. This ranking is descriptive rather than
evidence of statistically significant differences. The reported test metrics
come from one holdout split, five-fold grouped cross-validation results
and their fold-to-fold standard deviations are available in the individual
result logs and provide context on variability, but do not replace repeated
independent test splits or uncertainty intervals for the differences between
models.

### Permutation importance

The following charts show mean permutation importance from five-fold,
molecule-solvent-pair-grouped cross-validation for each model variant. Values
are the increase in validation RMSE after shuffling a feature and larger
positive values indicate greater predictive contribution in that fitted model.
Error bars show the fold-to-fold standard deviation. Negative or near-zero
values indicate little or no consistent predictive contribution and are not
evidence of a statistically significant effect. Basic variants use the row-
wise training dataset but still calculate these importance values using
grouped cross-validation. Since feature sets differ between variants, compare
feature rankings within each panel with care.

![Random Forest grouped-CV permutation importance](results/random_forest_permutation_importance.png)

![Extra Trees grouped-CV permutation importance](results/extra_trees_permutation_importance.png)

![CatBoost grouped-CV permutation importance](results/catboost_permutation_importance.png)

The results of the random-forest-regressor permutation importance show that
all models result in a very similar order and values of the descriptors
importance. In the models trained with solvent categories the solvent has by
far the largest importance. This is expected due to the type of solvent being
the largest factor when determining solubility. The second most important
descriptor and the most important descriptor in the models using solvent
descriptors is the molar mass. This is higher than expected because in
discussions about solubility it is usually not considered before polarity
which is the next most important descriptor. The ring_count descriptor is
also unexpectedly high in this list likely due to the high correlation
with molecular weight. Temperature has a lower than expected importance
ranked just under the ring_count. This can be due to the dataset having a lot
of data measured at room temperature without any variance resulting in a lower
importance. The importances of the models using solvent descriptors are roughly
the same as the previous two models with the exception of the "solvent"
importance being divided onto the solvent descriptors making molecular weight
the most important descriptor. The solvent descriptors show in general a lower
importance than similar molecule descriptors. The highest solvent descriptor is
sol_log_P followed by sol_tpsa and because most solvents in the used whitelist
are relatively small molecules making descriptors other than log_P and tpsa less
important in comparison. This order changes slightly when training on all entries
of the dataset making tpsa slightly more important than log_P. This is likely a
result of the solvents previously filtered out being comparatively larger and
more complicated molecules. For those solvent tpsa might be a more important
descriptor than log_P.

The extra-trees-regressor results show similar trends, however notable
differences are the increased importance placed on specifically ring_count
descriptors regarding proticity (H-acceptors/donors) and less importance being
placed on molecular_weight. This is in line with proticity being commonly
referred to as an important factor when estimating solubility. These models
however also show a slightly lower accuracy.

The catboost-regressor results show an increased importance placed on log_P
ahead of molecular weight which is also in line with the current scientific
consensus. These models also follow similar trends than the random-forest-
regressor and extra-trees-regressor models.

A surprising result of these models is that temperature does not have a high
importance in all models placing in the lower half. This is notable despite most
of the dataset entries being recorded at room-temperature.

## Conclusion and outlook

Machine-learning was successfully performed on the parsed datasets showing results
in line with scientific consensus.
The models all successfully improved upon the baseline and adding the group
shuffle significantly decreased accuracy in all cases. The other modifications
like using solvent descriptors and expanding to all entries only showed small
differences in model accuracy. The different models placed the most importance on
different descriptors, like molecular weight (random-forest-regressor), ring count
(extra-trees-regressor) and log_P (catboost-regressor) while having similar trends
of placing polarity and proticity as more important descriptors than others. All
models showed in increased importance of tpsa when training on all dataset entries
instead of the filtered entries due to more complicated solvents being present in
the unfiltered dataset.

In the future this can be expanded by changing some descriptors like arom_ratio or
ring_count to a boolean data type to see the effects especially on the extra-trees
models.


## Model details

Random Forest, Extra Trees, and CatBoost are evaluated using MAE, RMSE, and
\(R^2\). The tree models use 400 estimators and `max_features=0.5`; CatBoost uses
400 iterations, learning rate 0.05, and depth 8. CatBoost handles the solvent
name as a categorical feature in the basic variants, while Random Forest and
Extra Trees encode it as one-hot features. Solvent-descriptor variants use
numeric solvent descriptors instead.

The parser stores canonical molecule and solvent identifiers so grouped
validation keeps each molecule-solvent pair together. Solvent descriptors do
not uniquely identify a solvent, so descriptor-based datasets use the
canonical `solvent_id` for grouping.

